    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A republic is a state without a monarch, governed as the “public thing”, res publica, by officials elected for limited terms who answer to the law and to the citizens.</li><li>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, most early republics were run by a narrow elite of patricians, nobles or merchants rather than by all their citizens.</li><li>Since the American and French revolutions the republic has become the usual form of the state: in 2017, 159 of the world’s 206 sovereign states had “republic” in their official names.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>San Marino, by tradition founded in 301, calls itself the world’s oldest surviving republic; its heads of state, two Captains Regent who rule jointly, still serve for only six months at a time.</p></aside>
    </section>

