    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">509 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">By Roman tradition the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a>, is expelled and the first two consuls are elected</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">287 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The lex Hortensia gives the decisions of the plebeian assembly the force of law for all Romans</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">27 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Octavian takes the name Augustus; the republic’s forms survive under the rule of one man</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1297</span><span class="rf-text">The Serrata restricts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>’s Great Council to the families already sitting in it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1588</span><span class="rf-text">The States General take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> for themselves, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1776</span><span class="rf-text">The Declaration of Independence rejects the British monarchy; the American states adopt republican constitutions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1792</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> abolishes the French monarchy and proclaims the First Republic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text">The German and Austrian monarchies fall and both countries are proclaimed republics</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-senate.jpg" alt="Roman Senate" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cicero_Denounces_Catiline_in_the_Roman_Senate_by_Cesare_Maccari.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cesare Maccari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A republic is defined first by what it lacks: a hereditary monarch. The state is treated as res publica, the “public thing” of its citizens rather than the property of a dynasty, and is run by officials who hold power for limited terms, usually by election, and act under laws they cannot change alone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> set the pattern. Its assemblies of citizens elected the magistrates, passed laws and decided on war and peace; the Senate, about 300 men, most of them former magistrates, advised the magistrates and in practice directed finance, foreign affairs and the provinces; and the magistrates governed. At their head stood two consuls elected for one year, each able to veto the other. Below them came the praetors, who commanded armies and administered justice, the censors, aediles and quaestors, and the tribunes of the plebs, whose veto could block any act of a magistrate. Offices were held for a year, shared by colleagues and climbed in a fixed order, the cursus honorum; in an emergency the consuls could name a dictator for at most six months.</p><p>Later republics varied this pattern widely but kept its principles: no permanent ruler, offices held for short terms and shared, and government bound by law. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> elected its doge for life but hedged him with councils; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> was a union of sovereign provinces whose delegates met in the States General. Most modern republics have a written constitution, an elected head of state, usually called president, and a separation of powers. In presidential republics such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the president also heads the government; in parliamentary republics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> a largely ceremonial president stands beside a prime minister answerable to parliament; semi-presidential republics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> divide executive power between the two.</p><p>A republic is not the same as a democracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, in The Spirit of the Laws of 1748, called a republic democratic when the whole people held supreme power and aristocratic when only part of it did, and most republics before the 19th century were aristocratic in this sense. Conversely, parliamentary monarchies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Scandinavian kingdoms, where elected representatives hold real power, are sometimes called crowned republics. The name alone guarantees little: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under the Assad family, power passed from father to son although neither state was formally a monarchy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="Cicero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Cicero_(1st-cent._BC)_-_Palazzo_Nuovo_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> res publica, “the public thing” or “public affair”. Roman writers used it for their state and its government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> used it to render the Greek politeia, the constitution of a city. In his dialogue De re publica he defined it as the property of a people, a people being not any crowd but a community joined by agreement on law and by common interest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> had already used politeia for one particular constitution, a mixture of oligarchy and democracy that he counted among the best forms within reach.</p><p>According to the Roman tradition recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, the Romans expelled their last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a>, in 509 BCE, after his son raped the noblewoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia">Lucretia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> and Lucretia’s husband Collatinus became the first consuls. Modern historians doubt the details and the early lists of consuls, and many see a more gradual change from kingship to rule by annual magistrates, but the Romans remembered the expulsion as the birth of their liberty, and the word rex became a reproach. The early republic was dominated by patrician families. In the Conflict of the Orders the plebeians won concessions by refusing service and withdrawing from the city: tribunes of their own after the first secession in 494 BCE, a written code, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a>, traditionally of 451–450 BCE, access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> from 367 BCE, and in 287 BCE the lex Hortensia, which gave the decisions of their assembly the force of law. A new nobility of patrician and plebeian families who had held high office then ruled.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was not alone. Phoenician cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> were at times governed by judges, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffete">suffetes</a>, instead of kings, and Aristotle admired the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, which had two annual suffetes, a council of elders and a popular assembly. In northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, perhaps from the 6th century BCE, gana-sanghas such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> were governed by assemblies of clan heads, though the evidence is scattered and scholars disagree about how widely power was shared. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, writing in the 2nd century BCE, explained Rome’s rise by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixed-constitution">mixed constitution</a>, in which the consuls stood for monarchy, the Senate for aristocracy and the people for democracy, each checking the others.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus.jpg" alt="Tiberius Gracchus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus">Tiberius Gracchus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean-Baptiste Claude Eugène Guillaume, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> did not survive its own success. Conquest brought provinces, armies loyal to their generals and fierce competition within the elite. The tribune <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus">Tiberius Gracchus</a> was killed in 133 BCE, and in 121 BCE the Senate passed its first “final decree” against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Gracchus</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and made himself dictator, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with his army in 49 BCE, and after his murder the lex Titia of 43 BCE handed the state to a triumvirate. In 27 BCE Octavian took the name Augustus. Romans went on calling their state res publica, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> still did, but power lay with the emperor.</p><p>Republics returned in the trading cities of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In northern and central Italy, where imperial authority was weak, towns formed communes and governed themselves through councils and elected officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, ruled by elected doges from the early 8th century, closed its Great Council in 1297 to the families then sitting in it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> was governed by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> under the Ordinances of Justice of 1293 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">Medici family</a> came to dominate it from 1434. Writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a> was published in 1531, revived the Roman language of liberty. Elsewhere the Swiss cantons and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free imperial cities</a> ruled themselves, as did Novgorod until 1478 and the Icelandic Commonwealth, which had no king, from 930 to 1262.</p><p>Larger republics followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, founded when the rebel provinces vested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in their States General in 1588, became a great power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was a Commonwealth from the execution of Charles I in 1649 until 1660. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> produced the first large republic of modern times: the Declaration of Independence of 1776 rejected monarchy, the former colonies adopted republican constitutions of their own, and the federal Constitution guaranteed every state “a Republican Form of Government”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> proclaimed a republic in September 1792 and set up sister republics across Europe, while its general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> ended the old republics of Venice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in 1797 and in 1804 made himself emperor; the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> became republics in the 1810s and 1820s. In 1900 France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and San Marino were Europe’s only republics, but Portugal followed in 1910, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1912, and the fall of the Russian, German, Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman empires turned most of Europe republican.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni.jpg" alt="Leonardo Bruni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walker_Art_Gallery,_Leonard_Bruni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixed-constitution">mixed constitution</a> gave it a stability that no simple form could match, because each part checked the others, and that this stability let Rome bring almost the whole known world under its rule in less than fifty-three years. Romans themselves prized the protections it gave the citizen, such as the right to appeal to the people against a magistrate’s sentence and the help of the tribunes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, writing De re publica in the 50s BCE, praised the balanced constitution of the ancestors as the best form of state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> writers renewed the case. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a>, drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, held that free republics bred greater men than monarchies, which stifled virtue, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> argued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a> that a people was wiser and more constant than a prince, that the quarrels of the Senate and the plebs had kept Rome free, and that republics, able to draw on many citizens of different temperaments, could adapt to changing times better than a single ruler.</p><p>Defenders pointed to the record. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> kept its republican constitution for five centuries after the Serrata without a successful coup, and writers such as Gasparo Contarini, whose book on its magistrates appeared in 1543, held it up as a model of stability. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, with about 1.5 million people, became a great power in the 17th century and controlled a worldwide network of trade; its relative religious toleration drew refugees, merchants and thinkers, and its Golden Age produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-vermeer">Johannes Vermeer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</a>. The regents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, a closed oligarchy, nonetheless spent heavily on canals, almshouses and hospitals.</p><p>Modern republicans have stressed liberty. In The Federalist No. 10 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> argued that a large representative republic would refine popular passions and control faction better than a small democracy. The historian of political thought Quentin Skinner and the philosopher Philip Pettit describe a distinct republican idea of freedom as non-domination: a people that lives at the mercy of a ruler, however kind, is not free, so citizens must share in making the laws that bind them. The German historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-von-ranke">Leopold von Ranke</a> wrote in 1848 that by founding a republic on the rights of the individual the Americans had spread the idea that power should come from below.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri.jpg" alt="Dante Alighieri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bargello_-_Kapelle_Fresko_2a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Critics have long held that republics are fragile and seldom equal. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s centuriate assembly, which elected the consuls, the richest citizens at first held 98 of the 193 voting units, voting stayed weighted by wealth after a reform in the 3rd century BCE, and only those who could reach Rome could vote at all. The republic’s last century was one of political murder and civil war, and the Senate’s “final decree” of 121 BCE set a precedent for killing citizens in the name of the state. Its medieval successors were mostly ruled by small councils entered by wealth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> membership or birth, and the excluded rose often: more than 200 urban revolts are recorded in the late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> the wool workers seized power in the Ciompi revolt of 1378. Factional strife drove the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a> into exile from Florence in 1302, and many Italian republics fell to lords; Florence itself became a duchy under the Medici in 1532.</p><p>Theorists drew conclusions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a> mocked republican liberty in Leviathan in 1651: the word Libertas was written in great letters on the turrets of Lucca, he observed, yet no citizen there had more freedom than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> thought republics workable only in small states, and the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>’s personal rule as Lord Protector, persuaded many that republics led to anarchy or tyranny. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> saw in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> “Imbecility in the government; discord among the provinces; foreign influence and indignities”; in 1672 an Orangist mob lynched its leading statesman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-de-witt">Johan de Witt</a>, and his brother Cornelis, and by the mid-18th century its stadtholder was a monarch in all but name. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a> let a single deputy break up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, and the oligarchy of great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> could not prevent the partitions of 1772–1795.</p><p>Modern republics have also broken down. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a> about 17,000 people were sentenced to death during the Terror of 1793–1794 before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> took power; the Second Republic ended in a coup by its own president, Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte, in 1851, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> in Hitler’s dictatorship. In much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> himself doubted that the new states were ready for liberal republics, military strongmen, the caudillos, often overrode the elections their constitutions promised.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montesquieu.jpg" alt="Montesquieu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Montesquieu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Jacques-Antoine Dassier, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic has become the ordinary form of the modern state. In 2017, 159 of the world’s 206 sovereign states had “republic” in their official names, from parliamentary republics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to presidential republics across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, though not all of them hold free elections. Italy chose a republic by referendum in 1946, with 54.3 per cent of the vote, and most former colonies became republics at or soon after independence. Some constitutions protect the form itself: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> has forbidden any amendment of the republican form of government since 1884, and Italy’s constitution of 1948 places it beyond constitutional revision. Older republics survive in miniature. San Marino, which by tradition was founded in 301, still elects two Captains Regent every six months, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> still call themselves Republic and Canton within the Swiss federation.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s vocabulary lives on. The authors of The Federalist signed themselves Publius, after a founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, like many other countries, gave its upper house the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>. Historians and political theorists still debate the republican tradition. Hans Baron described a “civic humanism” born of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>’s struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> around 1400; J. G. A. Pocock’s The Machiavellian Moment of 1975 traced a line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> through English republicans such as James Harrington to the American founders, and Bernard Bailyn and Gordon Wood argued that republican ideas of virtue and corruption shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> as much as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>’s liberalism, a view that Isaac Kramnick and Joyce Appleby contested. Paul Rahe has stressed how far the ancient republics, with their slaves and citizen-soldiers, differed from modern ones.</p><p>The Roman Republic itself is judged in different ways. Fergus Millar argued that its assemblies and public meetings gave it a real democratic element, while many historians see an oligarchy in which a few noble families competed for the votes of the people. Most agree that its institutions, from the Senate to the veto and the separation of offices, supplied the models that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and the framers of the American and French constitutions adapted.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>A republic is a state without a monarch, governed as the public’s affair by officials elected for limited terms under law. Born by tradition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 509 BCE, revived by the city-states of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and spread by the American and French revolutions, it was usually run by elites before the 19th century and is now the most common form of state.</em></p>

